*A Vignette about Architecture and the Image*

For most of architecture's history, the building came first, and the image followed. A building was designed, constructed, occupied, and then subsequently photographed. While a physical building is owned and used, a photograph of it is able to isolate, define, interpret, exaggerate, or even invent a cultural value for it. Increasingly, that sequence can work in reverse. The anticipated photograph can begin to shape the room that will eventually produce it.

The Museum of Ice Cream is perhaps the clearest example of this reversal. Its plastic sprinkle pool, pink subway car, oversized gag candy, and monochromatic rooms are immediately recognizable and designed for easy photography. Each environment establishes a simple interaction between a person and a space. Enter the pool. Stand beside the pop-art object. Take the picture. The visitor does not necessarily need to understand the organization of the building to understand what to do inside it. The relationship is simple and has clear intentions for proper engagement.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/molly-m-1.jpg" alt="Two Instagram profile screenshots, an aerial map of the Museum of Ice Cream's block at Broadway and Prince Street, and a color-coded plan of its rooms from the entrance lobby to the sprinkle pool, slide and play space" ancho="completo" /%}

The photograph is therefore no longer simply documentation of the architecture. Producing it has become part of the architectural program. The room is experienced by the person inside of it, of course, but the resulting image can then reach an audience far beyond the building. Here, architecture becomes both a physical experience and a mechanism for producing its own circulation.

Montez Press Radio operates almost in reverse. Its studio is small and relatively ordinary: microphones, tables, chairs, equipment, cables, shelves, and most importantly, people. Rather than constantly changing the architecture, Montez changes what happens within it. The same room can accommodate interviews, readings, performances, conversations, and parties. The room itself does not need to circulate because the broadcast does.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/molly-m-2.jpg" alt="The montezpressradio Instagram tagged photos and flyer grid, an aerial map marking Montez Press near Wooster and Grand Street, and a dimensioned floor plan of the shared space" ancho="completo" /%}

This gives a small physical space a much larger effective footprint. Someone does not have to enter Montez Press Radio to participate in what happens there. A broadcast can be heard elsewhere and archived online afterward. The architecture provides the conditions for producing media without necessarily becoming the primary image or product being consumed.

Meow Wolf occupies a position somewhere between these two conditions. Omega Mart in Las Vegas begins with something immediately recognizable: a supermarket. Shelves, products, signs, refrigerators, and fluorescent lighting establish a familiar environment, but openings within the store lead into an increasingly complicated network of rooms, corridors, installations, and fictional spaces.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/molly-m-3.jpg" alt="The meow__wolf Instagram tagged photos and grid, an aerial map of Convergence Station's parcel between the I-25 and I-70 viaducts in Denver, and the hand-drawn visitor map of its floors" ancho="completo" /%}

Almost everything inside Omega Mart can be photographed, but no individual photograph adequately explains the whole. A strange product, glowing corridor, artificial landscape, or enormous machine can circulate independently online without revealing how it connects to everything around it. Unlike the Museum of Ice Cream, where a room can become a relatively complete image, Meow Wolf produces fragments. The photograph gives enough information to generate interest while withholding much of the spatial experience.

These three institutions therefore suggest different relationships between architecture and media. Museum of Ice Cream constructs rooms that become photographs. Montez Press Radio constructs a room from which media travels outward. Meow Wolf creates an environment that produces endless images while resisting complete representation by any one of them.

Architecture has always been mediated through photographs, magazines, television, exhibitions, and other forms of representation. What has changed is who produces those representations and how quickly they circulate. The architectural image no longer belongs only to the architect, institution, or professional photographer. Visitors continuously produce and distribute their own versions of a space.

The question is therefore not simply whether architecture has become "Instagrammable." It is what happens when circulation itself becomes something architecture is expected to produce. In these spaces, the image is no longer just what comes after architecture. Increasingly, it is one of the conditions through which architecture is conceived, occupied, and understood.
